<InsightArticle title="Choosing an AI coding tool starts with where you review the change" description="Compare editor, terminal and GitHub coding assistants with a small repository task and a practical review process." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="7 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="Compare editor, terminal and GitHub coding assistants with a small repository task and a practical review process.">
<p>Our frozen AIRH catalogue contains 268 language models after its suffix-based variant filter. That gives you a list of models, but it cannot tell you which coding application will fit your working day.</p>
<p>A coding tool decides how you supply files, approve commands and inspect changes. The underlying model contributes to the answer, but the application determines much of the work around that answer. Someone maintaining a small website inside an editor has different needs from someone leaving a repository task to run while they attend a meeting. Buying the same model name does not make those experiences interchangeable.</p>
<p>This guide compares ways of working rather than declaring a winner. We read the linked product documentation on 14 November 2026. We have not run a comparative coding test, measured completion rates or checked every subscription entitlement. The catalogue figures below describe our records, not the set of models offered by any particular coding tool.</p>
<h2>The catalogue counts models, while the purchase buys an application</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto"><table><caption>Frozen AIRH catalogue, 14 November 2026. Selections are defined on the transparency page.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Selection</th><th scope="col">Records</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Language-model entries</th><td>291</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">After suffix-based variant filtering</th><td>268</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">With release date and positive context window</th><td>252</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The table shows why a model directory is an awkward shopping list for coding software. Even the active, priced selection says nothing about editor integration, command approval or the quality of a proposed patch. A model can appear in our catalogue without being offered by the application you want. The application's own model selector and current documentation settle that question.</p>
<p>Start with the place where you already read changes. If you spend your day inside an editor, a tool that presents modifications beside the files may reduce the effort of following its work. If your work centres on shell commands and repository scripts, a terminal assistant may feel more natural. If colleagues discuss changes through GitHub issues and pull requests, a service that works in that setting deserves a look.</p>
<p>These are starting points for a trial, not fixed categories. Products increasingly offer several interfaces. The important question is which interface you will actually use, with your repository, your permissions and your review habits. A feature listed on a marketing page is less useful than a change you can understand before accepting it.</p>
<h2>The editor keeps the proposed change beside the code</h2>
<p><a href="https://cursor.com/docs">Cursor's documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes code understanding, planning, changes and review. That makes an editor-centred workflow a sensible candidate for someone who wants to move between reading a file and discussing a modification without continually changing applications. It does not establish that Cursor produces better code than another product.</p>
<p>For a first exercise, choose an ordinary bug with a visible symptom. Perhaps a search field loses its value when you open a result and return. Write down the current behaviour and the desired behaviour before opening the assistant. Include the command that runs the project and the existing check that matters. A vague request to improve the website makes it harder to judge whether the tool followed instructions.</p>
<p>Ask for an explanation of the likely cause before authorising the edit. The explanation should identify relevant files and a plausible path from the bug to the proposed repair. It need not be long. If the assistant cannot connect its suggested change to the symptom, adding more generated code is unlikely to make review easier.</p>
<p>Then read the diff, meaning the view of added and removed lines. Pay attention to unrelated files as well as the intended repair. A small bug that produces a dependency upgrade, formatting across the repository and a rewritten configuration has become a larger job. You can ask the assistant to reduce that scope before accepting anything.</p>
<h2>Terminal access makes existing project commands part of the conversation</h2>
<p><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview">Claude Code's overview</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes a tool that reads code, edits files and runs commands, with terminal and other interfaces. For a repository with useful build and test scripts, that is a reason to trial it against the commands you already trust. It is not a reason to grant unrestricted access immediately.</p>
<p>The terminal can be intimidating when you are learning, and a friendly explanation does not change what a command does. Before approving a command, you should be able to tell whether it reads files, changes files, installs software or contacts a service. When that distinction is unclear, ask for an explanation and a narrower command. A tool that fits your experience should help you understand the work it proposes.</p>
<p>Existing tests provide a useful boundary. Ask the assistant to reproduce the bug with the project's own test runner, make the smallest relevant repair, and run the same check afterwards. A passing result supports a specific statement about that check. It does not establish that the application is free of defects or that the test actually covers the reported symptom.</p>
<p>Some tasks are easier to judge by running the application. A repaired keyboard shortcut needs trying with the keyboard. A changed form needs an invalid input as well as a valid one. The assistant's final message should distinguish what it executed from what it merely inspected. That distinction is useful regardless of which product you choose.</p>
<h2>GitHub work needs a handover a colleague can review</h2>
<p><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/copilot-surfaces/copilot-on-github">GitHub's Copilot documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes repository questions, planned changes and review on GitHub. Availability depends on the plan and organisation policy. A repository-centred service is worth considering when the natural unit of work is an issue followed by a pull request.</p>
<p>The issue needs enough context to stand alone. State the failing behaviour, the intended result and any files or areas that should stay out of scope. Include reproduction steps that another person can follow. The assistant should not have to infer a private conversation that never reached the repository. Clear handovers also help human colleagues, so the writing is useful even if the tool disappoints.</p>
<p>Review the resulting pull request as you would a colleague's first attempt. Read the description, inspect the changed lines and compare the checks with the original request. If a screenshot or running example would resolve uncertainty, request it. A confident summary and a green badge can coexist with a misunderstanding of the task.</p>
<p>For private repositories, the organisation's rules may decide the shortlist before personal preference does. The permitted service, account type and repository access matter. This guide has not audited vendor contracts or your employer's policy. The person responsible for that repository should confirm what may be connected before a trial uses its code.</p>
<h2>A modest repair exposes more than a polished demonstration</h2>
<p>Use one bounded task to compare the workflows you are considering. Save the starting revision, use separate branches and keep the written request unchanged. Record where you had to intervene: finding files, explaining the project, correcting an assumption or reducing the patch. These observations tell you where the application helped and where it created work.</p>
<p>The useful outcome is a repair you can explain and maintain. Count a result as unfinished if you cannot run it or understand its important changes. That is not a claim that one product is incapable. It means this attempt did not yet satisfy the job you set. A follow-up request can resolve it, and the extra work belongs in your notes.</p>
<p>Subscription prices and usage allowances need checking at the point of purchase. API token prices in our model catalogue are not subscription quotes. A bundled plan can include limits, different models or separate charges for particular features. Before committing, find the usage screen and confirm what your trial consumed. The bill and the review experience belong in the same decision.</p>
<p>Choose the tool whose changes you can follow in the setting where you work. Keep the trial branch and its notes as a useful reference for the next renewal. If another product later looks promising, you already have a concrete task to revisit instead of relying on somebody else's demonstration.</p>
